  "publicacion": {
    "titulo": "The inflation report has a hole in it. Here is why.",
    "descripcion": "The BLS October 2026 CPI report shows prices up 0.4% in one month (CPI-U, seasonally adjusted). But the yearly inflation rate is missing: prices were never collected in October 2025, during the government shutdown, so October 2026 has nothing to be compared against. Instead, the Fed watches the short trend: core prices rose at about a 3.7% annualized pace over the last three months (July to October 2026). That is why a December rate hike is being discussed. One hot month is noisy, and any yearly figure for October you read today is an estimate, not an official number. The yearly comparison returns with next month's report. Not financial advice. Sources: BLS CPI-U and core CPI series (CUSR0000SA0, CUSR0000SA0L1E); Kiplinger, Nov 2026.",
